  "marked": false,
  "answer": "<p>The <b>third option</b> is the most appropriate: <b>Since this is a production environment, best practice is to maintain the RDS instance external to Elastic Beanstalk. The web application should connect to the database when it launches.</b> This approach aligns with AWS best practices for production. Externalizing the RDS instance keeps the database lifecycle independent of the Beanstalk environment, ensuring that redeployments, environment terminations, or updates (such as immutable deployments) do not inadvertently delete or modify your persistent data store.</p><ul><li><i>You should add the RDS PostgreSQL instance to Elastic Beanstalk via the database configuration options in the console, providing the appropriate credentials and instance details.</i> – This method is suitable for development or testing but not for production, as the RDS instance can be deleted if the Beanstalk environment is terminated.</li><li><i>It is not feasible to connect a production Elastic Beanstalk environment to an RDS instance.</i> – This is incorrect; Elastic Beanstalk applications can connect to external RDS databases using connection parameters.</li><li><i>Elastic Beanstalk does not support adding PostgreSQL RDS databases, so you cannot link one to the environment.</i> – This is not true; PostgreSQL is supported by AWS RDS and can be connected to from Beanstalk.</li></ul><p><b>Tip:</b> For all environments, especially production, always create RDS instances outside of Elastic Beanstalk and connect your app via configuration files or environment variables. This prevents accidental data loss during app or environment modifications.</p>",
  "query": "<p>Your development team is leveraging AWS Elastic Beanstalk to deploy web applications in a production setting.<br>The project must interact with a PostgreSQL RDS database that is running in a separate production environment.<br>Additionally, the Elastic Beanstalk environment uses immutable deployment, which means new instances are created with each deployment.<br>Given these circumstances, which approach should you recommend for your Elastic Beanstalk application to properly connect to the RDS database?</p>",
